---
import Page from '../../layouts/Page.astro';

const title = 'KeeForge vs KeePassium — Two Native iOS KeePass Clients Compared';
const description = 'A fair comparison between KeeForge and KeePassium for iOS — features, pricing, open-source posture, and when each is the right choice.';
---

<Page
    lang="en"
    enPath="/vs/keepassium/"
    dePath="/de/vs/keepassium/"
    frPath="/fr/vs/keepassium/"
    esPath="/es/vs/keepassium/"
    zhHansPath="/zh-hans/vs/keepassium/"
    zhHantPath="/zh-hant/vs/keepassium/"
    jaPath="/ja/vs/keepassium/"
    itPath="/it/vs/keepassium/"
    ruPath="/ru/vs/keepassium/"
    title={title}
    description={description}
    eyebrow="Comparison"
    heading="KeeForge vs KeePassium"
    lead="Both are native iOS apps for opening KeePass .kdbx databases. They overlap on the basics and diverge on a few important points — pricing, hardware key support, and feature scope. Here's a fair side-by-side."
>
    <section class="vs-section">
        <h2>The short version</h2>
        <p>
            KeePassium is a mature, well-respected iOS KeePass client with a strong free tier and an optional Premium upgrade that unlocks advanced features (notably YubiKey hardware key support). It has a multi-year track record and a careful, security-focused team behind it.
        </p>
        <p>
            KeeForge is a newer, fully-free alternative — every feature is in the free tier, the codebase is fully open source under GPLv3, and there is no premium plan. It targets iOS 18+ and is built natively for current Apple platform features.
        </p>
        <p>
            Both apps read the same <code class="mono">.kdbx</code> file format, so you can keep the same vault and try them side-by-side without committing.
        </p>
    </section>

    <section class="vs-section">
        <h2>Feature comparison</h2>
        <p class="vs-table-note">As of writing. Features change — check both vendors' release notes for the latest.</p>
        <div class="vs-table-wrap">
            <table class="vs-table">
                <thead>
                    <tr>
                        <th></th>
                        <th>KeeForge</th>
                        <th>KeePassium</th>
                    </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr>
                        <td>Open KeePass <code class="mono">.kdbx</code> databases</td>
                        <td>Yes</td>
                        <td>Yes</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td>Edit, create, and delete entries</td>
                        <td>Yes</td>
                        <td>Yes (Premium for unlimited; free has limits)</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td>AutoFill (passwords + passkeys + TOTP)</td>
                        <td>Yes</td>
                        <td>Yes</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td>Face ID / Touch ID unlock</td>
                        <td>Yes</td>
                        <td>Yes</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td>Cloud sync (iCloud, Dropbox, OneDrive, WebDAV, Files)</td>
                        <td>Yes</td>
                        <td>Yes</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td>YubiKey / hardware key support</td>
                        <td>Read-only, on iPhone over NFC</td>
                        <td>Yes (Premium)</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td>Subscription / paid tier</td>
                        <td>None — optional tip jar only</td>
                        <td>Free + Premium subscription</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td>Open source</td>
                        <td>GPLv3 (full app)</td>
                        <td>GPLv3 (free version)</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td>Minimum iOS version</td>
                        <td>iOS 18</td>
                        <td>Lower (supports older devices)</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td>Localization</td>
                        <td>English, German, French, Spanish, Italian, Russian, Japanese, Simplified &amp; Traditional Chinese</td>
                        <td>20+ languages</td>
                    </tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </div>
    </section>

    <section class="vs-section">
        <div class="two-col">
            <div class="col-card">
                <h3>What KeePassium does well</h3>
                <ul>
                    <li>Multi-year track record with a careful, security-focused team.</li>
                    <li>Premium tier offers YubiKey hardware key support — useful if your threat model includes physical-key second-factor.</li>
                    <li>Localized into 20+ languages.</li>
                    <li>Supports a wider range of older iOS versions.</li>
                    <li>Strong reputation and recognition in the privacy-focused community.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>
            <div class="col-card">
                <h3>Where KeeForge differs</h3>
                <ul>
                    <li>Completely free. No subscription, no Premium tier, no in-app purchases beyond a voluntary tip jar.</li>
                    <li>Every feature is open-source under GPLv3 — there's no paid tier with closed components.</li>
                    <li>Built natively for iOS 18+ with current platform features (TOTP AutoFill, passkey AutoFill, Files-app integration) as first-class concerns.</li>
                    <li>Smaller, more focused feature surface — fewer settings to dig through, faster startup.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>
        </div>
    </section>

    <section class="vs-section">
        <h2>When to choose each</h2>
        <div class="two-col">
            <div class="choice-card">
                <h3>Choose KeeForge if&hellip;</h3>
                <ul>
                    <li>You don't want a subscription, ever.</li>
                    <li>You don't need to edit YubiKey-protected databases (KeeForge opens them read-only on iPhone over NFC).</li>
                    <li>You want a fully open-source app — every feature, not just the free tier.</li>
                    <li>You're on iOS 18 or later.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>
            <div class="choice-card">
                <h3>Choose KeePassium if&hellip;</h3>
                <ul>
                    <li>You need full YubiKey support: editing, wired keys, iPad, or Mac.</li>
                    <li>You're on an older iOS device (pre-iOS 18).</li>
                    <li>You want the most established option with the largest user base.</li>
                    <li>You need a language KeeForge doesn't ship yet.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>
        </div>
        <p class="closing-note">
            You don't have to pick one. Because both apps read standard <code class="mono">.kdbx</code> files, you can install both, point them at the same supported vault location, and decide which fits your day-to-day flow.
        </p>
    </section>

    <p class="see-also">Comparing more than two apps? See <a href="/best-keepass-app-iphone/">the best KeePass apps for iPhone</a>, with KeePassium, Strongbox, PanicVault and OneKeePass side by side.</p>

    <section class="vs-cta">
        <h2>Try KeeForge</h2>
        <p>Free, open-source, no accounts.</p>
        <div class="cta-row">
            <a href="https://apps.apple.com/app/id6759309295" class="app-store-badge" aria-label="Download on the App Store">
                <img src="https://tools.applemediaservices.com/api/badges/download-on-the-app-store/black/en-us?size=250x83" alt="Download on the App Store" height="52">
            </a>
            <a href="/" class="ghost-link">Back to homepage</a>
        </div>
    </section>
</Page>
